# Architecture Documentation Maintenance

The folder `docs/architecture/` is the living, detailed architecture reference for the Engine. It contains one file per topic area, listed in `docs/architecture/index.md`. Each file documents internal implementation details: database schema, type definitions, behaviour signatures, event flows, configuration, and relationships between subsystems.

## When to update

Update the **one** architecture file that owns the change. Do not also paste the same paragraph into `AGENTS.md`, `common-pitfalls.md`, and a decision entry.

Update `docs/architecture/` whenever:

- You modify code that changes the architecture (new types, renamed modules, changed event wiring, new endpoints, new tables, moved responsibilities)
- You gain significant new understanding of an engine subsystem through research — even if you did not edit any code
- You discover that existing documentation is incorrect, incomplete, duplicated, or one-off history
- Update `AGENTS.md` **only** when you add, rename, or remove: `evil:*` extension elements, supported BPMN element types, validator rules, FEEL context bindings, or umbrella apps. Do not reprint architecture tables there.
- Update `docs/architecture/api.md` when API endpoints are added, removed, or their request/response contracts change

For a lookup table mapping "what changed" to "which file to update", see the `architecture-docs` skill.

## What to write

- Concrete, technical content: table schemas, behaviour signatures, endpoint tables, event flow descriptions, configuration options
- Keep it factual and reference-oriented — this is not a tutorial, it is a technical reference for agents
- Overview: 3–4 sentences. Point to other files; do not copy their tables
- The document is written in English. Maintain that language.

## Adding new topics

When documenting a subsystem that does not fit any existing file, create a new file in `docs/architecture/` and add it to the topic list in `docs/architecture/index.md`.

## Special-purpose files

- **`common-pitfalls.md`** — Recurring constraints someone could hit **again**. Gate: “Would a competent person hit this without this note?” If no, it does not belong here. Format: title = the constraint; Mistake / Why / Correct approach (3–8 lines); optional short snippet. No dates, no “Fixed (…)”, no named failing tests as headlines, no CI logs, no investigation novels. Test-harness and CI facts go in `docs/architecture/testing.md`.

`docs/poc/ImplementationPlan.md` and `docs/poc/ImplementationPhases.md` are **archival**. Do not append to them. Do not treat them as the decision log or the phase tracker.

## What NOT to do

- Do not delete still-accurate facts
- Do rewrite, merge, or drop content that is wrong, duplicated across files, or one-off history (incident reports, “what we tried”, Phase banners, dead roadmap links)
- Do not append a session strip to the bottom of a file
- Do not paste test postmortems into `common-pitfalls.md`
- Do not duplicate information already in `docs/Architecture.md` or the user handbook (those cover overview and usage; `docs/architecture/` covers implementation detail)
- Do not require `AGENTS.md` + architecture + pitfalls for the same change. Pick the single file that owns it.
